<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
		The push entitlement. Without it the app receives no remote notifications at all — neither
		message pushes nor the VoIP pushes that ring an incoming call — and there was no entitlements
		file in this project before now, which is to say push has never actually been entitled.

		`development` targets Apple's sandbox APNs gateway. A release build must be signed with
		`production`, and the server must be pointed at the matching gateway (PHEME_APNS_PRODUCTION):
		a token minted against one gateway is rejected by the other with BadDeviceToken, which is the
		single most common reason a VoIP push silently never arrives.

		Which is why this is no longer a literal. It was hardcoded to `development`, so a release or
		TestFlight build shipped asking for the sandbox gateway and every push to it would have come
		back BadDeviceToken — with nothing in the app to say why. APS_ENVIRONMENT is set per build
		configuration in the Runner target: development for Debug and Profile, which are signed with
		a development profile, and production for Release.
	-->
	<key>aps-environment</key>
	<string>$(APS_ENVIRONMENT)</string>

	<!--
		The App Group the app shares with its NotificationServiceExtension, and the reason an
		iPhone can show a message rather than "New message".

		A preview is decrypted ON THE DEVICE — the server ships MLS ciphertext it cannot read
		itself — so the extension needs two things the app owns: the sealed MLS state, which
		MlsStore already writes into this group's container as a file, and the small data key
		that unseals it, which lives in this group's keychain access group.

		This must also be registered in the Apple Developer portal and be present in the
		provisioning profile. Until it is, the keychain calls that name the group fail with
		errSecMissingEntitlement — which MlsStore treats as "no previews on this device" rather
		than as an error, so an unprovisioned build still stores its keys and still works.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>group.tech.rh1.pheme</string>
	</array>

	<!--
		Communication notifications: what puts the SENDER'S AVATAR on a message notification, as a
		circular portrait beside their name, the way Messages and WhatsApp do.

		There is no field for this. An avatar reaches an iOS notification only by attaching an
		INSendMessageIntent to the content, and `UNNotificationContent.updating(from:)` requires
		this entitlement — without it the call throws and the notification renders with the app
		icon instead. Android reaches the same result through Person/MessagingStyle, which needs no
		equivalent permission; this is the iOS price for the same picture.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.usernotifications.communication</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
